<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.8.1: Area Diagrams</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01867</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.8.1: Area Diagrams</md:title>
    <md:uuid>84e82697-e793-420e-986e-b73e4560a691</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">
          <media alt="A rectangle of width 6 units is divided horizontally into sections that are 3 units long and 4 units long. The labels 3 and 4 are along the left side of the rectangle and 6 is across the top.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_02_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Explain why the diagram shows that <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00004">The entire diagram represents the product <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. The upper half represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> while the lower half represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00005">Draw a diagram to show that <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00006">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00008">Diagrams will vary, but should show a large rectangle with two sub-rectangles, with one sub-rectangle representing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and the other representing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. For example:</para>
            <para id="para-00009">
                      <media alt="A rectangle of width 5 units is divided horizontally into sections that are x units long and 2 units long. The labels x and 2 are along the left side of the rectangle and 5 is across the top.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_02_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
            
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
